#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd4abd5d-2642-5f0d-af2f-5ed5c265526a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/id #uuid "2b2f2b8e-4625-5550-bb37-5ab9ca409c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-5032" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$[math]\\phantom{2x+20}[/math]; x - 42x^{2} + 12x + 25; 2x^{2} - 8x_(―); 4x^{2} -[math]\\phantom{20x+25}[/math]; 4x^{2} -20x - 80_(―); 4x^{2} + 26x105$" :question/worked-solution "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n2x + 20; x - 42x^{2} + 12x + 25; 2x^{2} - 8x_(―); 4x^{2} -20x + 25; 4x^{2} -20x - 80_(―); 4x^{2} + 26x105\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are $2x+20$ and $20x+25$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c342f1-881b-5f3e-8c76-448eec165168" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial With Some Zero Coefficients by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/id #uuid "bda2e91a-5b55-5deb-8232-7e79fabf6f29" :question/math-academy-id "e-5033" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$x + 1[math]\\phantom{x^2 - x + 1}[/math]; x + 1x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x + 64; x + 1x^{3} + 0x^{2}_(―); x + 1x^{3}- 0x^{2} + 0x + 64; x + 1x^{3}- 0x^{2} - 0x_(―); x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} -[math]\\phantom{x + 64}[/math]; x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} -x + 11_(―); x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} + x -63$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x^2$-term and the $x$-term. So, we write it using zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3+{\\color{red}0}x^2 + {\\color{red}0}x + 64\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$x + 1x^{2} - x + 1; x + 1x^{3} + 0x^{2} + 0x + 64; x + 1x^{3} + 0x^{2}_(―); x + 1x^{3}- 0x^{2} + 0x + 64; x + 1x^{3}- 0x^{2} - 0x_(―); x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} -x + 64; x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} -x + 11_(―); x + 1x^{3} - x^{2} + x -63$\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\qquad$ $x^2 - x + 1\\quad$ and $\\quad x + 64$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bdfd8fc-ce8d-5950-870d-c0570ef755bf" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Result of Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e3e171d1-4428-5a4d-a050-5ad8eb843aff" :question/math-academy-id "e-5110" :question/problem "Consider the following polynomial division:\n$x + 22x - 2; x + 52x^{2} + 18x - 15; x + 22x^{2} + 10x- 15_(―); x + 23x^{2}- 12x - 15; x + 23x^{2}- 12x - 10_(―); x + 23x^{2} - 2x -15$\n\nFrom the above, what is $\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5}$?" :question/worked-solution "$$\nx + 22x - 2; x + 52x^{2} + 18x - 15; x + 22x^{2} + 10x- 15_(―); x + 23x^{2}- 12x - 15; x + 23x^{2}- 12x - 10_(―); x + 23x^{2} - 2x -15\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $2x^2+8x -5$ by the linear binomial ${\\color{purple}x+5}$ gives\n\n- the quotient ${\\color{blue}2x-2}$, and\n- the remainder ${\\color{red}5}$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x^2+8x-5}{x+5} = {\\color{blue}2x-2}+\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{\\color{purple}x+5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd4abd5d-2642-5f0d-af2f-5ed5c265526a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8880d8d2-c546-5a02-a342-2b0309748345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x + 2$ and $6x - 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125841} {:answer/id #uuid "9d026acc-402d-5ba4-a2d1-59f7eded970a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x + 1$ and $6x - 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186125842} {:answer/id #uuid "a5742d09-1d55-54ea-b38b-cfa5222464ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2x + 2$ and $3x - 1$" :db/id 